<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nok terracottas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 900 BCE–1st century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nok terracottas are fired-clay sculptures of the Nok culture of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, named after the village of Nok in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaduna-state">Kaduna State</a>, where the first was found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> workings in 1928. Hollow and nearly life-size, they show human heads and figures with stylised features, detailed hairstyles and abundant jewellery; the earliest may date from about 900 BCE. Excluding ancient Egyptian art, they are regarded as the earliest large figurative sculpture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Their function is unknown, and over 1,000 have been illegally dug up and smuggled abroad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nok-terracottas.jpg" alt="Nok terracottas" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FA2010 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Africa_Nok_Head_Kimbell.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
